\section{Methods}
\label{sec:methods}

\subsection{Overview}
The UKG-SE v3.0 system is implemented as an autonomous, hardware-accelerated
simulation engine composed of three primary subsystems: (1) the Solver Core,
(2) the Hamiltonian Phase-Space Engine, and (3) the Secure State Ledger.
All experiments were conducted using a continuous-yield execution loop in
which each subsystem contributes to real-time stability evaluation and
self-refinement.

\subsection{Tensor Field Computation}
Tensor fields $T^{\mu\nu}$ were generated using a stochastic numerical
procedure intended to emulate multi-domain physical inputs. For each cycle,
a $4\times4$ tensor was produced:
\[
T^{\mu\nu} \in \mathbb{R}^{4\times4}.
\]
The covariant residual was computed using a discrete gradient operator:
\[
R = \left\lVert \nabla_{\mu} T^{\mu\nu} \right\rVert.
\]
A stability threshold $R_{\mathrm{crit}}$ was defined at runtime. When
$R > R_{\mathrm{crit}}$, the tensor field was mutated according to
\[
T^{\mu\nu}_{\mathrm{new}} = \alpha T^{\mu\nu}_{\mathrm{old}},
\qquad \alpha = 0.98,
\]
representing contraction of unstable tensor pathways.

\subsection{Hamiltonian Phase-Space Integration}
Phase-space coordinates $(q,p)$ were initialized as
\[
(q_{0}, p_{0}) = (1.0, 0.5).
\]
The Hamiltonian was defined as
\[
H(q,p) = \frac{p^{2}}{2} + V(q),
\qquad V(q) = \frac{q^{2}}{0.5}.
\]
Trajectories were integrated using a first-order explicit update:
\[
q_{n+1} = q_{n} + \Delta t\, p_{n}, \qquad
p_{n+1} = p_{n} - \Delta t\, q_{n},
\]
with $\Delta t = 0.1$.

A divergence metric was defined as
\[
D = |q\,p|.
\]
When $D > D_{\mathrm{crit}}$, the trajectory was marked as unstable and
pruned from the simulation.

\subsection{Autonomous Mutation and Pruning Logic}
Each simulation cycle evaluated both tensor stability and phase-space
divergence. Two Boolean flags were recorded:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\mathrm{mutated} = \mathrm{True}$ if $R > R_{\mathrm{crit}}$,
    \item $\mathrm{pruned} = \mathrm{True}$ if $D > D_{\mathrm{crit}}$.
\end{itemize}
These flags were used to quantify the frequency of autonomous refactoring
events across the full simulation run.

\subsection{Secure State Ledger}
All cycle outputs were recorded in an append-only JSON Lines ledger.
Each entry contained:
\begin{itemize}
    \item tensor field $T^{\mu\nu}$,
    \item residual $R$,
    \item phase-space coordinates $(q,p)$,
    \item mutation and pruning flags,
    \item timestamp $\tau$,
    \item SHA-3 hash.
\end{itemize}

The hash was computed as
\[
\mathrm{Hash}(E) = \mathrm{SHA3}\big(\mathrm{JSON}(E)\big),
\]
ensuring tamper-evident integrity. Ledger entries were persisted to disk
for offline statistical analysis.

\subsection{Simulation Regimes}
Two stability regimes were tested:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Strict regime:} $R_{\mathrm{crit}} = 0.2$, $D_{\mathrm{crit}} = 0.5$,
    \item \textbf{Relaxed regime:} $R_{\mathrm{crit}} = 0.8$, $D_{\mathrm{crit}} = 1.5$.
\end{enumerate}
Each regime was executed for $N=3$ cycles, and mutation/pruning frequencies
were compared to evaluate sensitivity to stability constraints.

\subsection{Implementation}
All methods were implemented in Python using NumPy for tensor operations
and SHA-3 hashing for ledger verification. The full evolution loop is
summarized as:
\[
\mathrm{Cycle} =
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
T \rightarrow R \rightarrow T_{\mathrm{mut}} \\
(q,p) \rightarrow D \rightarrow \mathrm{prune}(q,p) \\
\mathrm{Ledger}(T,R,q,p,\mathrm{flags})
\end{array}
\right.
\]
This loop defines the autonomous behavior of UKG-SE v3.0.